  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: ClinCare 2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "Your husband is participating in a clinical trial. Although the treatment
    is working, you notice he\u2019s been unusually fatigued and nauseated over the
    last week, but he downplays it to his care team.\n\n<b>Since the treatment seems
    to be working, it\u2019s better not to bring up mild side effects. The research
    team only wants to know about serious ones.</b>\n\nIs this <b>true</b> or <b>false</b>?"
  explanation: "Caregivers should always report any change, no matter how small.
    Even mild or seemingly unrelated side effects can provide valuable information
    that helps researchers evaluate a treatment\u2019s safety and tolerability. Their
    observations can even help identify early warning signs or patterns that patients
    might not notice themselves, ensuring that the research team has a complete picture
    of how the treatment affects daily life."
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Caregiver
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Clinical
